{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Model Weight Space.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Model Weight Space.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"model weight space model weight space","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Model Weight Space.md","fileName":"Model Weight Space.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Model Weight Space","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 02:18\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Machine Learning and Neural Networks]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Machine Learning and Neural Networks","Model Input Space","Loss Function","Gradient Descent","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Model Weight Space.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Model Weight Space.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"model weight space model weight space","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Model Weight Space.md","fileName":"Model Weight Space.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Model Weight Space","heading":"Model Weight Space","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Weight space is the set of possible parameter combinations for a model. If a model has two trainable weights, each point in a two-axis weight space specifies one candidate weighted mapping. Moving in this space changes the model, not the input examples in [[Model Input Space]].\n\nTraining searches for a point whose model fits the examples well. An [[Loss Function|error or loss function]] can assign a score to each point, creating an error surface over weight space; [[Gradient Descent]] uses local changes in that score to guide parameter updates. Kelleher uses this geometric view to connect a simple linear model to neural-network training.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Machine Learning and Neural Networks","Model Input Space","Loss Function","Gradient Descent","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properti